# 2D Enemy Patrol and Chase AI

A simple enemy can move between waypoints and chase a target inside a range. This sample uses Rigidbody2D and Transform positions for target detection; it does not implement line-of-sight occlusion.

## Setup
Create an enemy with Rigidbody2D and Collider2D. Assign two waypoint Transforms and a target. Freeze Z rotation if the enemy should stay upright. Choose whether the enemy moves by physics or by Transform; this example uses Rigidbody2D.MovePosition.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class EnemyPatrolChase2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointA;
        [SerializeField] private Transform pointB;
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float patrolSpeed = 2f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float chaseSpeed = 3f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float detectionRange = 5f;

        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private bool towardB = true;

        private void Awake() => body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            Vector2 current = body.position;
            bool chasing = target != null &&
                ((Vector2)target.position - current).sqrMagnitude <= detectionRange * detectionRange;

            Vector2 destination;
            float speed;
            if (chasing)
            {
                destination = target.position;
                speed = chaseSpeed;
            }
            else
            {
                if (pointA == null || pointB == null) return;
                destination = towardB ? (Vector2)pointB.position : (Vector2)pointA.position;
                speed = patrolSpeed;
            }

            Vector2 next = Vector2.MoveTowards(current, destination, speed * Time.fixedDeltaTime);
            body.MovePosition(next);

            if (!chasing && Vector2.Distance(next, destination) <= 0.05f)
                towardB = !towardB;
        }
    }

## Extensions
Add a line-of-sight raycast for walls, a return-to-patrol delay, attack range and cooldown, and explicit states when behavior becomes complex. Do not allow a separate movement script to also move the same Rigidbody2D.

## Common mistakes
- Waypoints are missing.
- Rigidbody2D settings conflict with direct Transform changes elsewhere.
- Chase range is checked using distance from the wrong origin.
- Enemy chases through walls because only distance is checked.

# 2D Sprite Animation and Animator

## Import and create clips
1. Set sprite sheet Texture Type to Sprite (2D and UI), Sprite Mode to Multiple, then Apply.
2. Open Sprite Editor, slice the sheet, then Apply.
3. Select sliced sprites in order and create an animation clip.
4. Keep Pixels Per Unit and sprite pivots consistent to prevent size changes and wobble.
5. Inspect Animation window keyframes and remove accidental keys on properties the clip should not control.

## Animator setup
Add Animator and assign an Animator Controller. Create parameters such as Speed (Float), Grounded (Bool), and Jump (Trigger). Use explicit transition conditions. Disable Has Exit Time for immediate locomotion changes; enable it when a clip should finish first. A Blend Tree can smoothly blend idle/walk/run. Names and types must match exactly.

## Parameter driver
Save as SpriteAnimatorDriver.cs and attach to the Animator object.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class SpriteAnimatorDriver : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Animator animator;
        private static readonly int SpeedHash = Animator.StringToHash("Speed");
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (animator == null) animator = GetComponent<Animator>();
        }
        public void SetMoveSpeed(float speed)
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetFloat(SpeedHash, Mathf.Abs(speed));
        }
        public void SetGrounded(bool grounded)
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetBool("Grounded", grounded);
        }
        public void TriggerJump()
        {
            if (animator != null) animator.SetTrigger("Jump");
        }
    }

This drives parameters; it does not create states or transitions. Configure those in the Animator window.

## Sorting and troubleshooting
SpriteRenderer uses Sorting Layer and Order in Layer. For top-down games, sorting by Y may be useful. Check for missing controllers, misspelled parameters, wrong parameter types, disabled Loop Time, inconsistent pivots, and Animator/gameplay code both writing the same Transform property.

# 2D Sprite Flipping and Aiming

## Flip a character horizontally
Use SpriteRenderer.flipX when only the rendered sprite needs to face the other direction. This avoids changing Transform scale and introducing negative-scale side effects.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class SpriteFacing2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private SpriteRenderer spriteRenderer;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float deadZone = 0.05f;

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (spriteRenderer == null) spriteRenderer = GetComponentInChildren<SpriteRenderer>();
        }

        public void SetHorizontalMove(float x)
        {
            if (spriteRenderer == null || Mathf.Abs(x) < deadZone) return;
            spriteRenderer.flipX = x < 0f;
        }
    }

Call SetHorizontalMove with the character's horizontal movement input or velocity. If the character has a weapon/child transform that must also face the aim direction, flip or rotate that transform separately.

## Aim toward mouse
For a 2D world on the XY plane and a standard camera facing that plane, convert the mouse to world coordinates. Use camera-space depth and check the returned coordinate plane. A simple screen-to-world conversion must use a depth that intersects the gameplay plane.

    Vector3 mouse = Camera.main.ScreenToWorldPoint(
        new Vector3(Mouse.current.position.ReadValue().x,
                    Mouse.current.position.ReadValue().y,
                    Mathf.Abs(Camera.main.transform.position.z)));
    Vector2 aim = (Vector2)mouse - (Vector2)weapon.position;
    if (aim.sqrMagnitude > 0.0001f)
        weapon.right = aim.normalized;

This snippet assumes a conventional 2D camera at negative Z and gameplay around Z = 0. It requires the Input System namespace and a Mouse device. For gamepad aiming, use a direction vector from the stick instead.

## Common mistakes
- Sprite is mirrored but colliders/child weapon are not positioned as intended.
- Transform scale is negated and creates hierarchy side effects.
- Mouse depth does not intersect the gameplay plane.
- Code rotates the whole Rigidbody root and changes physics behavior.

# 2D Audio: Music and Sound Effects

AudioListener is usually on the camera; keep one active listener. AudioSource plays clips and configures volume, pitch, looping, spatial blend, and mixer routing. AudioMixer groups sources and controls effects/volume centrally. For conventional 2D effects, set Spatial Blend to 0.

## One-shot service
Create an AudioService object, add an AudioSource, disable Play On Awake, and assign a short confirm clip. Save this as AudioService.cs.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class AudioService : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public static AudioService Instance { get; private set; }
        [SerializeField] private AudioSource source;
        [SerializeField] private AudioClip confirmClip;
        [SerializeField, Range(0f, 1f)] private float volume = 1f;
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (Instance != null && Instance != this)
            {
                Destroy(gameObject);
                return;
            }
            Instance = this;
            if (source == null) source = GetComponent<AudioSource>();
        }
        public void PlayConfirm()
        {
            if (source != null && confirmClip != null)
                source.PlayOneShot(confirmClip, volume);
        }
    }

Call AudioService.Instance?.PlayConfirm() from UI/gameplay code. This sample does not persist across scenes. If adding DontDestroyOnLoad, prevent duplicates and deliberately manage lifetime.

## Import and performance
Short effects may use Decompress On Load or Compressed In Memory depending on latency, memory, and CPU. Long music often suits streaming or compressed-in-memory settings. Profile on target hardware. Pool or cap sources for rapid repeated sounds.

Common mistakes: no listener, multiple listeners, no clip/play call, Spatial Blend incorrectly set, mixer muted, or playback occurring while audio is paused.

# 2D Camera Bounds and Level Confinement

A follow camera should stay inside level bounds. A simple clamp computes the camera's orthographic half-height and half-width, then limits its center position so the view stays inside the rectangle.

## Clamp an orthographic camera
Attach to the Camera or camera rig. Assign a target and world-space minimum/maximum bounds. Assumes a 2D orthographic camera and rectangular level bounds.

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Camera))]
    public sealed class CameraBounds2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 minBounds;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 maxBounds;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 offset;
        private Camera cam;

        private void Awake() => cam = GetComponent<Camera>();

        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (target == null || !cam.orthographic) return;

            float halfHeight = cam.orthographicSize;
            float halfWidth = halfHeight * cam.aspect;

            float minX = minBounds.x + halfWidth;
            float maxX = maxBounds.x - halfWidth;
            float minY = minBounds.y + halfHeight;
            float maxY = maxBounds.y - halfHeight;

            // When the view is wider/taller than the level, center it on that axis.
            float x = minX > maxX ? (minBounds.x + maxBounds.x) * 0.5f
                                  : Mathf.Clamp(target.position.x + offset.x, minX, maxX);
            float y = minY > maxY ? (minBounds.y + maxBounds.y) * 0.5f
                                  : Mathf.Clamp(target.position.y + offset.y, minY, maxY);

            transform.position = new Vector3(x, y, transform.position.z);
        }
    }

Set min/max to the actual world-space rectangle around the playable level, not the player's bounds. If using a separate smooth follow script, combine follow and clamping in one controller or ensure update order is intentional. Cinemachine Confiner may be preferable for irregular level boundaries.

## Common mistakes
- Using pixel bounds instead of world coordinates.
- Forgetting that camera half-width depends on aspect ratio.
- Clamping before smoothing, then smoothing moves the camera outside the bounds.
- The level is smaller than the camera view, requiring centering rather than an impossible clamp.

# 2D Camera: Follow, Framing, and Pixel Art

For an orthographic camera, Size is half the vertical world-space view height. Size 5 means 10 world units high; visible width depends on aspect ratio.

## Smooth follow
Save as CameraFollow2D.cs and attach to Main Camera. Assign the player Transform. A common convention is gameplay at Z = 0 and camera at Z = -10.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class CameraFollow2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform target;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float smoothTime = 0.12f;
        [SerializeField] private Vector2 offset = new Vector2(0f, 1f);
        [SerializeField] private bool followX = true;
        [SerializeField] private bool followY = true;
        private Vector3 velocity;
        private void LateUpdate()
        {
            if (target == null) return;
            Vector3 current = transform.position;
            Vector3 desired = new Vector3(
                followX ? target.position.x + offset.x : current.x,
                followY ? target.position.y + offset.y : current.y,
                current.z);
            transform.position = Vector3.SmoothDamp(current, desired, ref velocity, smoothTime);
        }
    }

LateUpdate is a common place for camera following. For physics-driven targets, test interpolation and consider Cinemachine.

## Pixel art checklist
- Use consistent Pixels Per Unit.
- Consider the 2D Pixel Perfect package/component.
- Match reference resolution and art PPU.
- Avoid fractional camera motion if crisp pixel alignment is required.
- Test target resolutions and aspect ratios.

A follow script does not constrain the camera to level bounds. Use Cinemachine confiners or calculate bounds using camera half-width and half-height. Use a separate rig/offset for shake so shake and follow do not fight.

Common mistakes: missing target, wrong Z convention, confusing Size with full view height, excessive smoothing, and sub-pixel blur.

# Inspector Attributes and Serialization Reference

Unity serializes supported fields on MonoBehaviours and ScriptableObjects. Ordinary C# properties are not automatically shown or serialized by the default Inspector.

## Common attributes

    [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    [Header("Movement")]
    [Tooltip("World units per second.")]
    [Min(0f)] [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;
    [Range(0f, 1f)] [SerializeField] private float volume = 1f;
    [TextArea] [SerializeField] private string notes;
    [Space] [SerializeField] private Transform target;
    [HideInInspector] public int runtimeCounter;
    [System.NonSerialized] public float transientValue;

- SerializeField: serialize a supported private field and show it in Inspector.
- Header: section heading.
- Tooltip: hover description.
- Min: indicates a minimum for supported Inspector workflows; validate in code when correctness is important.
- Range: numeric slider.
- TextArea: multiline text field.
- Space: visual spacing.
- HideInInspector: hide a serialized field from Inspector.
- NonSerialized: exclude a field from Unity serialization.

## Component requirements and editor commands

    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class NeedsBody : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [ContextMenu("Reset Runtime State")]
        private void ResetRuntimeState()
        {
            // Reset values for a convenient editor command.
        }
    }

RequireComponent helps ensure dependencies are present when the component is added, but runtime validation is still useful. ContextMenu exposes a method in the component Inspector menu. Avoid destructive actions that are easy to trigger accidentally.

## Renaming serialized fields
Use UnityEngine.Serialization.FormerlySerializedAs when renaming a field and preserving existing values matters.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.Serialization;

    public sealed class ExampleSettings : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [FormerlySerializedAs("oldSpeed")]
        [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    }

## Serialization rules
- Unity's built-in serializer supports a specific set of fields and collection patterns.
- Dictionaries, arbitrary properties, and many nested generic types are not supported directly by the default serializer.
- Avoid static fields for Inspector configuration.
- Serialized object references may become missing if assets/objects are deleted or moved.
- After changing serialized field names/types, inspect existing prefabs/scenes for lost data.

# C# Essentials for Unity Scripts

This is a Unity-focused reminder, not a complete C# language specification.

## Common types
- int: whole numbers and counters.
- float: decimal values used by most Unity math.
- bool: true/false state.
- string: text.
- Vector2 / Vector3: coordinates or directions.
- Quaternion: rotation representation.
- GameObject: scene object; Component: behavior attached to an object.
- Transform: position, rotation, and scale.
- List<T>: growable ordered collection; Dictionary<TKey,TValue>: keyed lookup collection.

## Fields, properties, and methods

    [SerializeField] private float speed = 5f;
    public int Score { get; private set; }

    public void AddScore(int amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0) return;
        Score += amount;
    }

SerializeField makes a private field editable in the Inspector. A property controls code access, but Unity's default Inspector serialization does not serialize ordinary properties. Use fields for serialized data and properties for runtime access when appropriate.

## Branching and loops

    if (health <= 0f)
    {
        // Handle death.
    }
    else
    {
        // Continue playing.
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < items.Count; i++)
    {
        Debug.Log(items[i]);
    }

Use return to exit a method early, break to exit a loop, and continue to skip to the next loop iteration. Check collection length before indexing.

## Null and reference safety
A Unity reference may be missing, destroyed, or not assigned. Check references before use:

    if (target == null)
    {
        Debug.LogWarning("Target is missing.", this);
        return;
    }

NullReferenceException means code accessed a member through a missing reference. Trace where it should have been assigned or created.

## Common misconceptions
- = assigns; == compares.
- && requires both conditions; || needs either condition.
- Integer division can discard the fractional part; use floating-point operands when needed.
- Arrays have fixed length; List<T> can grow.
- public does not guarantee that Unity serializes a field if its type/shape is unsupported.
- MonoBehaviour scripts normally attach to GameObjects; ScriptableObjects are asset-style data objects.
- Standard MonoBehaviour class names should match their C# filenames.

# Prefabs, ScriptableObjects, and Data

## Prefabs
A prefab is a reusable serialized GameObject hierarchy. Drag a configured scene object into the Project window to create one. Use prefabs for enemies, projectiles, pickups, effects, and repeatable UI.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class Spawner2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
        [SerializeField] private Transform spawnPoint;
        public GameObject Spawn()
        {
            if (prefab == null)
            {
                Debug.LogError("Assign a prefab in the Inspector.", this);
                return null;
            }
            Transform point = spawnPoint != null ? spawnPoint : transform;
            return Instantiate(prefab, point.position, point.rotation);
        }
    }

Instantiate creates a runtime instance; it does not create a Project asset.

## Prefab workflow
- Edit the prefab asset when every instance should inherit the change.
- Inspect Overrides for scene-specific differences.
- Use variants for related types that share a base.
- Avoid scene-only references in reusable prefab assets unless assigned at runtime.
- Keep required components and dependencies on the prefab where practical.

## ScriptableObjects
Use ScriptableObjects for shared authored definitions such as weapons, enemies, levels, and items.

    using UnityEngine;
    [CreateAssetMenu(menuName = "Game/Weapon Definition")]
    public sealed class WeaponDefinition : ScriptableObject
    {
        public string displayName = "Basic";
        [Min(0f)] public float damage = 10f;
        [Min(0f)] public float cooldown = 0.5f;
        public Sprite icon;
    }

Create the asset through the Project window's Create menu. ScriptableObject assets are shared authored data, not a player save system. Avoid mutating project asset values as if they were per-player progress.

## Choosing storage
- Serialized component fields: per-instance configuration.
- Prefab: reusable object/component setup.
- ScriptableObject: shared authored definitions.
- JSON or platform save API: runtime player progress.
- Static fields/singletons: convenient global access but can hide dependencies and complicate testing.

# Unity C# Lifecycle and Script Architecture

| Callback | Typical purpose | Important detail |
|---|---|---|
| Awake | Cache components and initialize local state | Do not assume other objects have run Start |
| OnEnable | Subscribe to events or enable actions | Runs on each enable |
| Start | Setup needing other objects' Awake initialization | Runs once before first frame update while enabled |
| Update | Frame-based input and non-physics logic | Frame rate varies |
| FixedUpdate | Physics-step work | Zero, one, or multiple times per rendered frame |
| LateUpdate | Camera follow after ordinary updates | Other-script ordering may still need design |
| OnDisable | Unsubscribe and disable actions | Can run many times |
| OnDestroy | Final cleanup | Other objects may already be gone |

Collision and trigger callbacks require correct colliders, Rigidbody configuration, and matching 2D/3D physics types.

## Good habits
- Cache components in Awake instead of searching every frame.
- Keep movement, health, UI, and audio responsibilities separate.
- Use private fields with SerializeField for Inspector configuration.
- Validate references and log clear errors.
- Subscribe in OnEnable and unsubscribe in OnDisable if re-enable cycles are possible.
- Capture short-lived input in Update or action callbacks, then pass it to physics logic.
- Do not rely on unspecified order between unrelated scripts.

## Component pattern

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class ExampleComponent : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Rigidbody2D body;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float moveSpeed = 5f;
        private void Awake()
        {
            if (body == null) body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            if (body == null) Debug.LogError("Rigidbody2D is required.", this);
        }
        private void Update()
        {
            // Read frame-based input here.
        }
        private void FixedUpdate()
        {
            if (body == null) return;
            // Apply physics changes here.
        }
    }

Update runs per rendered frame; FixedUpdate uses the fixed timestep in Time settings. Never assume they run one-to-one. Coroutines run on Unity's main loop and are not threads. WaitForSeconds is scaled by timeScale; WaitForSecondsRealtime is not.

# Tags, GameObject Layers, Sorting Layers, and LayerMasks

These systems have related names but different purposes. Choose the one matching the problem.

## Tags
Tags identify a GameObject with one label, such as Player, Enemy, or Checkpoint. Use CompareTag for comparisons. Tags are not a collision filter; they answer identity questions.

    if (other.CompareTag("Player"))
    {
        // Handle a player entering a trigger.
    }

The tag must exist and be assigned exactly as the code expects.

## GameObject Layers
Layers group objects for cameras, physics filtering, and raycasts. A Camera Culling Mask decides which layers it renders. The Physics 2D or 3D collision matrix controls which layer pairs can interact.

## LayerMask
LayerMask stores a set of selected layers as bits. In the Inspector, choose layer names through the mask dropdown. In code, LayerMask.GetMask("Ground", "Enemy") is safer than manually typing bit values. Ensure names exist and match capitalization.

## Sorting Layers
Sorting Layers and Order in Layer control 2D renderer draw order. They do not determine physics collision. Changing a GameObject's Layer will not fix SpriteRenderer order, and changing its Sorting Layer will not stop a collider from colliding.

## Example: raycast filter

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class LayerFilteredRay2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private LayerMask targetLayers;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float distance = 5f;

        public bool HasTarget()
        {
            RaycastHit2D hit = Physics2D.Raycast(
                transform.position, transform.right, distance, targetLayers);
            return hit.collider != null;
        }
    }

## Troubleshooting sequence
1. Verify the object's tag/layer in Inspector.
2. Check camera culling mask when objects do not render.
3. Check Layer Collision Matrix when physics callbacks do not fire.
4. Check the query's LayerMask when raycasts/overlaps miss.
5. Check Sorting Layer and Order in Layer when 2D sprites render behind the wrong object.

# Unity Time, Coroutines, and Invoke

## Time values
- Time.deltaTime: elapsed scaled time since the previous rendered frame. Use for frame-based movement.
- Time.fixedDeltaTime: fixed physics timestep. Use when computing per-physics-step motion.
- Time.time: scaled game time; stops advancing when timeScale is zero.
- Time.unscaledDeltaTime: frame time unaffected by timeScale; useful for pause UI.
- Time.timeScale: global scaled-time multiplier. Zero pauses scaled time, not every Unity system.

## Frame-rate-independent movement
For an object moved directly by Transform rather than a Rigidbody:

    transform.position += direction.normalized * speed * Time.deltaTime;

Without deltaTime, distance changes with frame rate. For Rigidbody movement, use its physics API in FixedUpdate instead of combining Transform movement and simulation.

## Coroutine example
Save as TimedEffect.cs.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class TimedEffect : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float duration = 1f;
        private Coroutine activeRoutine;

        public void Begin()
        {
            if (activeRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(activeRoutine);
            activeRoutine = StartCoroutine(Run());
        }

        private IEnumerator Run()
        {
            // Apply the effect here.
            yield return new WaitForSeconds(duration);
            // Remove the effect here.
            activeRoutine = null;
        }

        private void OnDisable()
        {
            if (activeRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(activeRoutine);
            activeRoutine = null;
        }
    }

WaitForSeconds uses scaled time. Use WaitForSecondsRealtime for real time even while paused. Coroutines are not threads; blocking work inside one still blocks the main thread.

## Invoke and cancellation
Invoke(nameof(MethodName), delay) schedules a delayed method; CancelInvoke cancels pending Invoke calls on that component. For sequences, cancellation, or data passed between steps, coroutines are often clearer.

# 2D Checkpoints and Level Progression

A checkpoint stores a respawn position after the player touches it. Keep checkpoint state separate from its visual indicator. Decide whether progress resets after scene reload or persists in a save file.

## Checkpoint trigger
Attach this script to an object with Collider2D set to Is Trigger. Assign a player tag and optionally a child respawnPoint Transform.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Checkpoint2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private string playerTag = "Player";
        [SerializeField] private Transform respawnPoint;
        [SerializeField] private GameObject activatedVisual;
        private bool activated;

        private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
        {
            if (activated || !other.CompareTag(playerTag)) return;

            PlayerRespawn2D respawn = other.GetComponentInParent<PlayerRespawn2D>();
            if (respawn == null) return;

            Transform point = respawnPoint != null ? respawnPoint : transform;
            respawn.SetCheckpoint(point.position);
            activated = true;
            if (activatedVisual != null) activatedVisual.SetActive(true);
        }
    }

## Player respawn component
Attach to the player root. Assign initialSpawnPoint if the initial spawn differs from the player's starting Transform.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class PlayerRespawn2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Transform initialSpawnPoint;
        [SerializeField] private Rigidbody2D body;
        private Vector2 checkpointPosition;

        private void Awake()
        {
            if (body == null) body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            checkpointPosition = initialSpawnPoint != null
                ? (Vector2)initialSpawnPoint.position
                : (Vector2)transform.position;
        }

        public void SetCheckpoint(Vector2 position) => checkpointPosition = position;

        public void Respawn()
        {
            if (body != null)
            {
                body.linearVelocity = Vector2.zero;
                body.angularVelocity = 0f;
                body.position = checkpointPosition;
            }
            else
            {
                transform.position = checkpointPosition;
            }
        }
    }

Call Respawn when health reaches zero. Decide separately whether to restore health, lives, and collected items. Using a nullable Transform instead of a zero-coordinate sentinel allows the world origin to be a valid spawn.

## Scene progression
Use SceneManager and include scenes in the active Build Profile. Store the next scene name/index deliberately; Project folder order does not determine build index.

## Common mistakes
- Trigger never fires because collider/layer/Rigidbody setup is wrong.
- Player tag does not match.
- Respawn leaves Rigidbody velocity intact.
- Health/death events are not connected to Respawn.
- Scene name or build index is not configured.

# 2D Damage, Knockback, and Temporary Invulnerability

This pattern uses a Rigidbody2D actor and a Health2D component. Call ReceiveHit only after a collision/weapon system confirms a hit. Keep damage validation separate from collision detection.

    using System.Collections;
    using UnityEngine;

    [RequireComponent(typeof(Rigidbody2D))]
    public sealed class DamageResponse2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private Health2D health;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float invulnerabilitySeconds = 0.6f;
        [SerializeField, Min(0f)] private float knockbackImpulse = 5f;
        private Rigidbody2D body;
        private bool invulnerable;
        private Coroutine invulnerabilityRoutine;

        private void Awake()
        {
            body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();
            if (health == null) health = GetComponent<Health2D>();
        }

        public void ReceiveHit(float damage, Vector2 sourcePosition)
        {
            if (invulnerable || health == null || health.CurrentHealth <= 0f) return;
            health.TakeDamage(damage);

            Vector2 away = (Vector2)transform.position - sourcePosition;
            if (away.sqrMagnitude < 0.0001f) away = Vector2.up;
            body.AddForce(away.normalized * knockbackImpulse, ForceMode2D.Impulse);

            if (invulnerabilityRoutine != null) StopCoroutine(invulnerabilityRoutine);
            invulnerabilityRoutine = StartCoroutine(InvulnerabilityWindow());
        }

        private IEnumerator InvulnerabilityWindow()
        {
            invulnerable = true;
            yield return new WaitForSeconds(invulnerabilitySeconds);
            invulnerable = false;
            invulnerabilityRoutine = null;
        }
    }

WaitForSeconds uses scaled time, so pausing the game pauses this timer. A visual blink effect can show invulnerability, but must not be the actual damage gate.

Common mistakes: health is on a parent but GetComponent checks only the same object, Rigidbody2D is missing, a timer restarts unexpectedly, or visual blinking is used instead of an invulnerability state.

# Health and damage system (2D)

This small reusable component tracks health, exposes a damage method, and raises an event when health changes. It does not assume a particular UI or respawn system.

## PlayerHealth.cs

```csharp
using System;
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class PlayerHealth : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField, Min(1)] private int maxHealth = 5;
    public int CurrentHealth { get; private set; }
    public int MaxHealth => maxHealth;
    public bool IsDead => CurrentHealth <= 0;

    public event Action<int, int> HealthChanged;
    public event Action Died;

    private void Awake()
    {
        CurrentHealth = maxHealth;
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }

    public void TakeDamage(int amount)
    {
        if (IsDead || amount <= 0) return;

        CurrentHealth = Mathf.Max(0, CurrentHealth - amount);
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);

        if (IsDead)
            Died?.Invoke();
    }

    public void Heal(int amount)
    {
        if (IsDead || amount <= 0) return;

        CurrentHealth = Mathf.Min(maxHealth, CurrentHealth + amount);
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }

    public void RestoreFullHealth()
    {
        CurrentHealth = maxHealth;
        HealthChanged?.Invoke(CurrentHealth, maxHealth);
    }
}
```

## Connecting a UI

Subscribe to HealthChanged in the UI's OnEnable and unsubscribe in OnDisable. Update the bar only when the event fires, rather than searching for UI objects every frame. Subscribe to Died to open a game-over screen or request a respawn.

## Design notes

- Clamp health so it never falls below zero or above maximum.
- Ignore damage after death unless your game explicitly supports it.
- Decide whether healing can revive a dead character.
- For multiplayer, validate damage and health changes on the authoritative peer/server.

# Object pooling for 2D effects

Pooling can reduce repeated Instantiate/Destroy overhead for projectiles, particles, and short-lived effects. Measure before adding complexity.

## Basic pool

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class Pool2D : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private GameObject prefab;
    [SerializeField, Min(1)] private int prewarm = 12;
    private readonly Queue<GameObject> pool = new();

    private void Awake()
    {
        if (prefab == null)
        {
            Debug.LogError("Assign a prefab.", this);
            enabled = false;
            return;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < prewarm; i++)
        {
            GameObject item = Instantiate(prefab, transform);
            item.SetActive(false);
            pool.Enqueue(item);
        }
    }

    public GameObject Get(Vector3 position, Quaternion rotation)
    {
        GameObject item = pool.Count > 0 ? pool.Dequeue() : Instantiate(prefab);
        item.transform.SetPositionAndRotation(position, rotation);
        item.SetActive(true);
        return item;
    }

    public void Return(GameObject item)
    {
        if (item == null) return;
        item.SetActive(false);
        item.transform.SetParent(transform);
        pool.Enqueue(item);
    }
}
```

## Reset state on reuse

A pooled projectile must reset velocity, damage, lifetime, collision state, and any coroutine/timer state when activated. Ensure an object cannot be returned to the pool twice. Consider Unity's ObjectPool<T> API for more advanced projects.

# 2D Pickups and Respawning

## Pickup setup
Create a pickup GameObject with SpriteRenderer and Collider2D. Enable Is Trigger. Add a Rigidbody2D set to Kinematic if needed for the desired trigger setup. Put the player on a layer that the pickup detects.

## Collect once
Save as Pickup2D.cs.

    using UnityEngine;

    public sealed class Pickup2D : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField, Min(0)] private int points = 1;
        [SerializeField] private string playerTag = "Player";
        [SerializeField] private GameObject visualRoot;
        private bool collected;

        private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)
        {
            if (collected || !other.CompareTag(playerTag)) return;
            collected = true;

            ScoreCounter score = FindFirstObjectByType<ScoreCounter>();
            if (score != null) score.Add(points);

            if (visualRoot != null) visualRoot.SetActive(false);
            Destroy(gameObject);
        }
    }

The example expects a ScoreCounter component; add the counter below. For a large game, prefer an explicit reference/event or player interface rather than searching the scene at collection time.

    using UnityEngine;
    public sealed class ScoreCounter : MonoBehaviour
    {
        public int Score { get; private set; }
        public void Add(int amount)
        {
            if (amount > 0) Score += amount;
        }
    }

Set the player tag to exactly Player in the Tag Manager and assign it to the player. Alternatively, use layers or a component/interface check. If you want a pickup to respawn, do not Destroy it; disable its visual/collider and start a coroutine to reactivate it after a delay.

## Respawn pattern
Keep the pickup GameObject active so its coroutine host survives; disable only the child visuals and collider. After collecting, wait, restore visuals/collider, and reset the collected flag. If the whole pickup GameObject is deactivated, its coroutine will not continue.

## Common mistakes
- Tag spelling/capitalization differs.
- Trigger collider has no valid physics pair.
- Pickup can award points multiple times due to missing collected guard.
- A coroutine host is deactivated when its timer needs to complete.
- Destroying the object prevents respawning that same instance.

# 2D Save and Load with JSON

Use Application.persistentDataPath for small local progress. This example intentionally stores only simple fields. It is not secure storage; players can edit local save files.

## Data model and system
Save as LocalProgressSave.cs.

    using System;
    using System.IO;
    using UnityEngine;

    [Serializable]
    public sealed class LocalProgressData
    {
        public int coins;
        public int bestScore;
        public int unlockedLevel = 1;
    }

    public sealed class LocalProgressSave : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private string fileName = "progress.json";
        public LocalProgressData Data { get; private set; } = new LocalProgressData();
        private string PathToSave => Path.Combine(Application.persistentDataPath, fileName);

        public void Save()
        {
            try
            {
                string json = JsonUtility.ToJson(Data, true);
                File.WriteAllText(PathToSave, json);
            }
            catch (Exception e)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Could not save progress: {e.Message}", this);
            }
        }

        public void Load()
        {
            if (!File.Exists(PathToSave))
            {
                Data = new LocalProgressData();
                return;
            }

            try
            {
                Data = JsonUtility.FromJson<LocalProgressData>(File.ReadAllText(PathToSave))
                       ?? new LocalProgressData();
            }
            catch (Exception e)
            {
                Debug.LogError($"Could not load progress: {e.Message}", this);
                Data = new LocalProgressData();
            }
        }

        private void OnApplicationPause(bool paused)
        {
            if (paused) Save();
        }
    }

Place one instance in a scene and call Load at startup and Save after meaningful progress changes. Mobile platforms may not call quit reliably, so don't depend only on OnApplicationQuit. Avoid using arbitrary player-controlled file names without validation.

## Serialization limits
JsonUtility handles serializable fields and supported types, but not arbitrary dictionaries or properties. Add a schema version and migration logic if the data format changes. Use stable item IDs rather than serializing direct references to scene objects.

## Testing
Test a first run with no file, normal save/load, invalid/corrupt JSON, and values at boundary cases. Local files are not appropriate for secrets or authoritative multiplayer currency.

# 2D Gameplay: Scene Changes, Pause, and Restart

Add every required scene to the active Build Profile. Scene names are case-sensitive; build indices depend on the configured scene list.

## Pause controller
Requires the Input System package. Attach to a game manager and assign a pause menu GameObject. Wire Resume and RestartCurrentScene to menu buttons.

    using UnityEngine;
    using UnityEngine.InputSystem;
    using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;
    public sealed class PauseController : MonoBehaviour
    {
        [SerializeField] private GameObject pauseMenu;
        private bool paused;
        private void Start() => SetPaused(false);
        private void Update()
        {
            if (Keyboard.current != null && Keyboard.current.escapeKey.wasPressedThisFrame)
                SetPaused(!paused);
        }
        public void Resume() => SetPaused(false);
        public void RestartCurrentScene()
        {
            Time.timeScale = 1f;
            SceneManager.LoadScene(SceneManager.GetActiveScene().buildIndex);
        }
        private void SetPaused(bool value)
        {
            paused = value;
            Time.timeScale = paused ? 0f : 1f;
            if (pauseMenu != null) pauseMenu.SetActive(paused);
        }
        private void OnDestroy() => Time.timeScale = 1f;
    }

Time.timeScale = 0 stops scaled physics and scaled waits, but Update still runs. WaitForSeconds uses scaled time; WaitForSecondsRealtime does not. Restore timeScale before scene changes. For gamepad/touch support, use a Pause action instead of hardcoding Escape. Avoid multiple systems independently controlling global timeScale.
